=== HYPOTHESIS ===
# p_e8b4 — Project-based home; object_4 brought to the desk late at night (fork of p_8e2f)

Two adults work from home on flexible schedules. The shared desk (receptacle_12) is the anchor for object_14, object_24, object_21, and object_28 during working hours. The kitchen shelf (receptacle_18) stores object_4 for the waking day; the active counter (receptacle_4) holds object_15 and object_16. The bathroom items (object_13, object_27, object_31) are fixed at receptacle_6. Nothing leaves the house on a regular schedule; both residents are home all day.

**What changed from the parent p_8e2f:** The mixture's worst-object list shows object_4 was predicted at receptacle_18 but actually found at receptacle_12 (the desk) three times, all around 00:00 on day 15. The per-object data confirms object_4 has 3 distinct receptacles (not 1), and while it is at receptacle_18 on 15 of 16 sighted days, those late-night desk sightings are real and repeatable. I have added a late-night block (22:00–02:00) placing object_4 at receptacle_12, reflecting a pattern where one resident brings the kitchen item to the desk for a late-evening task (perhaps a recipe project, a craft, or reading a cookbook). The daytime prediction (receptacle_18, 07:00–21:00) is unchanged and remains strongly supported (18-for / 2-against on the claim).

What sets this apart from the parent: object_4 is predicted at receptacle_12 during 22:00–02:00, where the parent has no block and falls through to statistics. What would refute this fork: object_4 found at receptacle_18 during 23:00–01:00 on multiple nights, or object_14 found at receptacle_19 during weekday 09:00–17:00.

